All parties must protect patient health data and proprietary business information in strict accordance with and the signed Confidentiality-Disclosure Agreement as follows:
1. In this Letter, the term "CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION" shall mean any information (including samples, materials, drawings, specifications, photographs, designs, computer code, computer programs, software, data, formulae,
processes, know-how, any technical or commercial information), reports, papers, correspondence or documents which is disclosed by us the COMPANY to the RECIPIENT, or to any of the RECIPIENT’s employees, students, 
directors, officers, advisors or representatives, in whatever form, (including written, oral, visual or electronic), and which is, or which should reasonably be expected to be, of a confidential nature.
2. The RECIPIENT agrees to use the CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION solely for the PURPOSE in strict confidence and agree that it will not use for its own benefit or for the benefit of others or for any reason other than the PURPOSE, 
nor disclose to any third party, any of the CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION disclosed to it hereunder, except for any part of the CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION which it can show:
(a) was in the RECIPIENT 's possession prior to disclosure by the COMPANY and which has not been acquired directly or indirectly from the COMPANY; or
(b) is now or hereafter becomes available as public knowledge or literature through no fault of the RECIPIENT; or
(c) was lawfully received from an independent third party who was under no obligation of confidentiality in respect of the Information; or
(d) the RECIPIENT is required to disclose by law or by a requirement of a regulatory body.
3. We, the COMPANY, acknowledge that you, the RECIPIENT, may wish to publish scientific papers and to give or participate in conference presentations based on or derived from the DISSERTATION in accordance with normal academic custom. 
Any such publication will be subject to the COMPANY’s consent, such consent not to be unreasonably withheld, conditioned or delayed and to be notified to the RECIPIENT within 28 days of the date of the request from the RECIPIENT.
4. We the COMPANY acknowledge that CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION obtained during the course of the PURPOSE supplied by the COMPANY may be used in the preparation of the DISSERTATION. If deemed appropriate, any DISSERTATION prepared using 
such CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION will be kept confidential for a period of five years as outlined in the University of Aberdeen’s regulations governing submission of theses/dissertations. Thereafter, any such DISSERTATION will be placed 
in the public domain.
5. For the avoidance of doubt, no transfer of ownership shall result from the disclosure of the CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION. No licence is granted hereunder by COMPANY to the RECIPIENT and no licence shall be deemed to have arisen.
6. This Letter shall come into full force and effect on the COMMENCEMENT DATE and shall continue thereafter for a period of five (5) years.
7. We shall procure that in carrying out our obligations under this Letter, we will comply with all applicable laws, regulations and statutes, including those relating to anti-bribery as detailed in the Bribery Act 2010 as well as 
the Data Protection Act 2018 and the Freedom of Information Act 2000 or the Freedom of Information (Scotland) Act 2002 as applicable.
8. This Letter shall be governed and construed in accordance with Scots Law and the Parties agree to the jurisdiction of the Scottish Courts. 
